[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Batch Processing and StatelessSession in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When dealing with enterprise applications, you often encounter use cases that require processing thousands or millions of records. Standard Hibernate operations rely on the first-level cache (Session), which can quickly lead to OutOfMemoryError exceptions when handling bulk inserts, updates, or deletes. This tutorial explores how to efficiently handle batch processing and introduces the StatelessSession interface for high-performance operations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem with Standard Sessions in Bulk Operations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, the Hibernate Session maintains a persistent context (first-level cache) for every managed entity loaded or saved within its lifecycle. When inserting 100,000 records sequentially using a standard Session, all 100,000 entity instances remain referenced in memory. To mitigate this, developers traditionally use session flushing and clearing in batches."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Traditional Batch Processing with Session.flush() and clear()"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent memory exhaustion during bulk inserts using a standard Session, you must periodically flush changes to the database and clear the session cache."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"StatelessSession session = sessionFactory.openStatelessSession();
Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();

for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    Customer customer = new Customer("Customer " + i);
    session.insert(customer);
    if (i % 50 == 0) {
        // Periodic flushing is not needed for stateless, but shown here conceptually for standard session
    }
}
tx.commit();
session.close();"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Introducing StatelessSession"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate provides the StatelessSession interface for command-style operations where data is streamed to or from the database without a persistent context. A StatelessSession is a lower-level abstraction that maps more closely to a direct JDBC connection."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No first-level cache: Entities are not read into or kept in memory."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No dirty checking: Changes to entities require explicit update statements."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No cascading operations: Associated entities must be handled explicitly."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No interceptors or event listeners: Lifecycle callbacks are bypassed."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Bulk Insertion Using StatelessSession"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example demonstrates how to insert a large number of records efficiently using StatelessSession without worrying about memory leaks from the persistence context."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"SessionFactory sessionFactory = configuration.buildSessionFactory();
StatelessSession statelessSession = sessionFactory.openStatelessSession();
Transaction transaction = statelessSession.beginTransaction();

try {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 50000; i++) {
        Product product = new Product();
        product.setName("Product Item " + i);
        product.setPrice(10.0 + i);

        statelessSession.insert(product);
    }
    transaction.commit();
} catch (Exception e) {
    if (transaction != null) transaction.rollback();
    e.printStackTrace();
} finally {
    statelessSession.close();
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Batch Processing"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure JDBC batch size in your properties file (e.g., hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50) to group SQL statements."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use StatelessSession primarily for ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) tasks and massive reporting queries."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always manage transactions explicitly when performing batch writes."}]
